#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qutebrowser userscript: fill web forms from a local TOML profile.

Usage (see ~/.config/qutebrowser/config.py for the `pf`/`pF` bindings):

    :spawn --userscript autofill                    # fill with default profile
    :spawn --userscript autofill --profile brasil    # fill with a named profile
    :spawn --userscript autofill --choose            # pick a profile via rofi
    :spawn --userscript autofill --overwrite         # also replace non-empty fields

Profiles live in ~/.config/qutebrowser/autofill/profiles.toml (created here as
a template, with obviously fake data, the first time this script runs and
finds it missing). $QUTE_CONFIG_DIR is honoured instead of the hardcoded path
when set (e.g. under `qutebrowser --basedir ...`), so this also works
unmodified against an isolated test profile.

How results are reported back to the user
------------------------------------------
Userscripts talk to qutebrowser by appending command lines to $QUTE_FIFO; they
have no channel to *read* a value back out of the browser (there is no return
path from `:jseval` to the spawning process). To still show a human-readable
"N fields filled" message, we lean on `:jseval` itself: when it is *not*
called with --quiet, qutebrowser's own jseval command already shows the
JS completion value via `message.info()` -- the exact same function the
`:message-info` command uses. So autofill.js's entry point computes and
*returns* the final message string (e.g. "autofill: 7 fields filled (profile
italia)"), and the second `:jseval` call below (no --quiet) is what ends up
shown to the user. No second round trip or global-variable polling is needed.

Privacy: isolated world, and no profile data ever touches disk
----------------------------------------------------------------
We run in `--world jseval`, qutebrowser's dedicated isolated JS world (the
same one `:jseval` itself defaults to), NOT `--world main`. In the main
world, a malicious page can monkeypatch builtins the page's own scripts and
ours would share (JSON.stringify/parse, Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor,
EventTarget.prototype.dispatchEvent, the HTMLInputElement value setter, ...)
and read the *entire* profile object as we hand it to QuteAutofill.run(),
including fields the form never even asked for (codice_fiscale, cpf, nif,
birth_date, ...). An isolated world gets its own pristine copies of those
builtins -- the page cannot see or intercept calls we make through them --
while DOM nodes themselves are still shared, so the native-setter-plus-
bubbling-events fill technique still works and is framework-compatible; this
is exactly how real browser extensions (e.g. password managers) autofill
React/Vue/Angular forms from isolated content scripts. See
autofill/tests/fixtures/react-tracker.html and monkeypatch-spy.html plus
tests/e2e_test.py for the fixtures that prove both properties empirically.

We send two separate `:jseval --world jseval` commands:
  1. `jseval --file --world jseval <path to the installed autofill.js>` --
     loads the (non-sensitive, static, never-changing) classifier/filler
     library fresh into that isolated world. No profile data here at all, so
     this is safe to read from a file (it always was just code).
  2. `jseval --world jseval QuteAutofill.run({...profile...}, {...opts...}).summary`
     -- a single physical line built directly with json.dumps() (which always
     backslash-escapes control characters, so it can never itself contain a
     raw newline) and sent straight over the FIFO. This is a valid JS object
     literal (JSON is a syntactic subset of JS), so no JSON.parse is needed.
     Nothing derived from the profile is ever written to disk: earlier
     revisions of this script wrote a per-run temp .js file containing the
     profile and deleted it a few seconds later, but avoiding the file
     entirely is strictly better, and qutebrowser's IPC preserves the
     ordering of same-frame runJavaScript calls issued back-to-back like
     this (verified empirically across many runs; if it ever didn't hold,
     step 2 would fail loudly with a ReferenceError, caught below and
     reported as "autofill error: ...", not a silent wrong fill).

`--world jseval` (vs. e.g. the "user" world, which is also isolated but is
documented as "currently unused" by qutebrowser and has no other advantage
here) was chosen because it is qutebrowser's own conventional world for
exactly this kind of one-off script evaluation, and because both jseval calls
in a single run need to land in the *same* isolated world so the second call
can see the QuteAutofill global the first one just defined.
"""

